---
// Body of the API page, below its title. Examples use real ids.
import { API, url } from '../../lib/site.js';
import { loadSite, apiExample } from '../../lib/data.js';

const { endpoints } = await loadSite();
const ex = await apiExample();
---
<p>
  Everything on this site comes from a public, read-only HTTP API that returns JSON. No account or key is needed.
  The site is for browsing and downloading whole files; the API is for everything else: filtering levels by quantum
  numbers, paging through transitions, or pulling data straight into a script.
</p>
<p>Base URL: <code>{API}</code></p>

<h2>How the data is organised</h2>
<p>
  A <strong>molecule</strong> is one isotopologue, identified by a slug such as <code>{ex.slug}</code>. Each molecule
  has one or more <strong>runs</strong>: a single MARVEL inversion of a published transition set, with its solved energy
  levels and the input transitions. Runs are never merged: when two runs exist for the same isotopologue (for example,
  different papers or MARVEL versions), both are listed and you pick one by its numeric id.
</p>
<p>The usual path is: list molecules, list a molecule's runs, then query that run's levels, transitions or files.</p>

<h2>Examples</h2>
<pre><code>{`# All molecules (isotopologues)
curl ${API}/molecules

# One isotopologue's runs, newest first, with ids, counts and file sizes
curl ${API}/molecules/${ex.slug}/runs

# Energy levels of a run, ordered by energy (first 1000 by default)
curl ${API}/runs/${ex.runId}/levels

# One level, by its full quantum-number string
curl "${API}/runs/${ex.runId}/levels?qn_key=${encodeURIComponent(ex.qnKey)}"

# All levels with one quantum number fixed
curl "${API}/runs/${ex.runId}/levels?qn.${ex.qnName}=${encodeURIComponent(ex.qnValue)}"

# Input transitions that were removed from the solve
curl "${API}/runs/${ex.runId}/transitions?removed=true"

# The raw published energy-levels file, saved under its original name
curl -O -J ${API}/runs/${ex.runId}/files/output_levels`}</code></pre>

<h3>From Python</h3>
<pre><code>{`import requests

API = "${API}"
runs = requests.get(f"{API}/molecules/${ex.slug}/runs").json()
levels = requests.get(f"{API}/runs/{runs[0]['id']}/levels", params={"limit": 10000}).json()
print(len(levels), levels[0]["energy"], levels[0]["quantum_numbers"])`}</code></pre>

<h2>Filtering and paging</h2>
<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>Quantum numbers.</strong> Each run lists its quantum-number names in <code>qn_names</code>
    (<code>GET /runs/&#123;id&#125;</code>). Filter levels on any of them with <code>qn.&lt;name&gt;=&lt;value&gt;</code>;
    combine several to narrow further. <code>qn_key</code> matches the full quantum-number string exactly. An unknown
    name returns an error listing the valid ones.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Transitions</strong> can be filtered by <code>source_tag</code> (the paper the line was measured in),
    <code>removed</code>, and <code>consistency_flag</code>. Endpoint levels are given as <code>upper</code> and
    <code>lower</code> quantum-number strings; they are <code>null</code> when that state was never solved.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Paging.</strong> Levels and transitions return at most <code>limit</code> rows (default 1000, maximum 10000),
    starting from <code>offset</code>. Keep increasing <code>offset</code> until a page comes back shorter than
    <code>limit</code>.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Whole files.</strong> For bulk data, the raw files (<code>output_levels</code>,
    <code>input_transitions</code>, <code>segment</code>) are faster than paging. They are the published files, byte
    for byte.
  </li>
</ul>

<h2>All endpoints</h2>
<div class="table-scroll">
  <table class="endpoints">
    <thead><tr><th>Endpoint</th><th>Description</th><th>Query parameters</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {endpoints.map((e) => (
        <tr>
          <td><code>GET {e.path}</code></td>
          <td>{e.description ?? e.summary}</td>
          <td>{e.params.length ? e.params.map((p) => <code>{p}</code>) : <span class="muted">—</span>}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
<p>
  The <a href={`${API}/docs`}>interactive API documentation</a> has every parameter and response in full, and lets you
  try requests in the browser. The machine-readable schema is at <a href={`${API}/openapi.json`}><code>/openapi.json</code></a>.
</p>
<p>If you use data from the API, please cite it as described on the <a href={url('/#cite')}>home page</a>.</p>

<style>
  .endpoints td:first-child { white-space: nowrap; }
  .endpoints td:last-child code { display: block; }
</style>
